C++ Object Slicing Demonstration
Description

This C++ program demonstrates object slicing in inheritance.

The program contains a Base class and a Derived class. A Derived object is passed to two functions: one by value and one by reference. Passing it by value causes object slicing, while passing it by reference preserves the complete derived object.

Classes Used
1. Base Class

The Base class is the base class.

It contains the virtual display() function.
The function displays "Base object".
It has a virtual destructor.
2. Derived Class

The Derived class inherits from the Base class.

It overrides the display() function.
The function displays "Derived object".
The override keyword is used to override the base-class virtual function.
Functions Used
1. displayByValue()
void displayByValue(Base object)

This function receives the object by value.

When the Derived object is passed to this function, only the Base portion is copied. This is called object slicing.

2. displayByReference()
void displayByReference(const Base& object)

This function receives the object by reference.

The complete Derived object is preserved, so the overridden Derived::display() function is called.

Main Function

In the main() function, a Derived object is created.

It is passed to both functions:

displayByValue(derived);
displayByReference(derived);

The results are then displayed.

Output
Passing by value: Base object
Passing by reference: Derived object
OOP Concept Used

Object Slicing

             Derived Object
                   |
          +--------+--------+
          |                 |
     Pass by Value     Pass by Reference
          |                 |
          ↓                 ↓
    Base portion       Complete object
          |                 |
          ↓                 ↓
    Base object       Derived object

When a derived object is passed by value as a base object, the derived portion is sliced off. Passing it by reference preserves the complete derived object.